    "text": "\n000\nFXUS65 KVEF 132339\nAFDVEF\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Las Vegas NV\n439 PM PDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n\n* Light scattered showers, gusty northwesterly winds, and cooler \n  temperatures expected today.\n\n* Gusty conditions return on Thursday and Friday with the chance\n  of precipitation returning early next week.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...Today through Monday.\n\nA closed area of low pressure and associated cold front will \ncontinue to push southeastward through our forecast area today  \nbefore it lifts eastward over the next few days. As a result,\nexpect light shower activity, particularly across southern \nNevada and northwestern Arizona. Not expecting much in the way of \nmeasurable rainfall, but can't rule out a few hundreths with \nshower activity over the higher terrain. Along with this light \nprecipitation, much cooler temperatures ushered into the region \nthis morning. Pioche, Nevada in Lincoln County and Tonopah, Nevada\nin Nye County received some light snowfall this morning as a \nresult. Otherwise, the region can expect high temperatures today \nbetween 15 and 20 degrees below seasonal normals. The daily record\ncool high for Las Vegas is in jeopardy this afternoon, with a \nforecast high of 65 and a standing record cool high of 66 degrees \nobserved in 1981. Goldfield, Nevada observed a low temperature of \n30 degrees this morning before warming up above freezing around 8 \nAM. As a result, the Freeze Warning for Esmeralda and central Nye \ncounties has been allowed to expire on time. Tonight into \nWednesday morning, there is a Freeze Warning for the northern \nOwens Valley including Bishop as well as for Lincoln County \nincluding Pioche and Caliente.\n\nAs the aforementioned closed low pushes eastward, it will leave\nthe Desert Southwest underneath a northwesterly flow aloft. A \nvorticity lobe will ride southeastward along this flow before \nultimately closing off over northern Baja / southeastern \nCalifornia / southwestern Arizona late-week. This will return \ngusty north winds to the region on Thursday, with gust speeds \nbetween 30 and 40 mph and locally higher speeds in the higher \nterrain. This closed low will remain in place through the weekend \nbefore weakening and pushing eastward for the start of the work \nweek. There is low confidence at the moment, but ensemble means \nsuggest that the placement of this low will help to usher tropical\nmoisture into the Mojave Desert from Hurricane Simon (currently \nprogged to travel up the Gulf of California), which will interact \nwith an additional incoming long-wave trough early next week. As a\nresult, there are currently widespread precipitation chances \nearly next week. Stay tuned as this forecast develops and \nconfidence increases through the week. \n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION...For Harry Reid...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Winds\ngenerally remain northerly through the forecast period, with some\nvariability between 320-020 true, especially this evening. Gusts \nto around 25KT will diminish early evening, with a brief lull \nexpected until late evening when intermittent gusts to 18-20KT are\nexpected to return. Gusts diminish mid- to late morning \nWednesday, with winds becoming light after sunset. Clouds \ngradually dissipate this evening, with clear skies tonight onward.\n\n\nFor the rest of southern Nevada, northwest Arizona and southeast\nCalifornia...For the 00Z Forecast Package...Light showers over\nnorthwestern Arizona and the southern Great Basin will diminish \nthis evening, with skies clearing areawide overnight. Winds across\nthe region generally remain northerly through the next 24 hours, \nwith some variability expected. Gusts to 25-30KT will continue \nearly in the forecast period, decreasing this evening, with \nanother round of gusts around 20KT expected to develop overnight \nfor Las Vegas Valley terminals. Elsewhere, gusts will hold off \nuntil mid- to late morning before resuming, with the strongest \ngusts to 25-30KT expected through the Lower Colorado River Valley \nfrom KIFP to KEED. The exception will be across the western Mojave\nDesert/KDAG, where elevated northeasterly winds this evening \nbecome light and variable overnight. \n\n&&\n\n.SPOTTER INFORMATION STATEMENT...Spotters are encouraged to report\nany significant weather or impacts according to standard operating\nprocedures.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...Soulat\nAVIATION...Phillipson\n\nFor more forecast information...see us on our webpage:\nhttps://weather.gov/lasvegas or follow us on Facebook and Twitter\n",
